  "query_preview": "..Shortwave is moving through the western part of the\nstate right now. Some early morning storms are initiating right\nnow and more will as the day progresses as there's still plenty of\nmonsoon moisture across the state for this wave to utilize. Since\nthe shortwave is in the western part of the state already, the\nmain focus for activity will be the eastern half of the state,\nmainly east of Interstate 17 and US-89. Once again the main\nthreats from these showers/storms will be heavy rain and isolated\nflash flooding as well as the potential for a few of these storms\nto reach severe levels with strong downburst winds and some\nquarter sized hail. Storms could potentially last into the\nnighttime hours tonight into Tuesday morning.\n\nThe rest of the week will remain pretty active as troughing over\nthe West Coast keeps rotating shortwaves across the area that \nwill be able to feed off the monsoon moisture in place. Coverage \nwill be dependent on how far east the trough sets up as well as \nthe activity from the previous day which could potentially work \nthe atmosphere over for the following day. So will have to go with\na general broad brush of shower and thunderstorm chances lasting \nthrough the week with better specifics the day of the event after \nseeing what the previous day did. The hazards mentioned in the \nprevious paragraph will certainly be possible each day.\n\nFriday afternoon into the weekend, it looks like the West Coast\ntrough advances eastward through the weekend, allowing a ridge to\nbuild to our west and put us under a northwest flow aloft. If \nthis scenario takes place, the monsoon moisture will be shoved out\nof the state and we'll be in a dry pattern this weekend into next\nweek. It's a bit early to say this is the end of the monsoon for\nthe year but it definitely will put a big pause to it if this does\nin fact pan out.",
